00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:04.519 align:start position:0%
the United States has filed new charges  against WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange  they accuse him of violating the  Espionage Act when he published secret  documents containing the names of  confidential military and diplomatic  sources Assange supporters call the  charges a threat to press freedom the US  Justice Department's new indictment says  that Julian Assange directed former Army  Intelligence analyst Chelsea Manning to  illegally obtain classified information  the WikiLeaks founder then released the  sensitive documents to escape being  extradited to Sweden on rape charges  Assange was granted asylum in the  Ecuadorian Embassy in London where he  stayed for seven years before the  embassy evicted him in April because of  disputes the Ecuadorians then allowed  London police to arrest Assange  he is currently serving a 50 week jail  sentence for breaching bail Sweden is  also asking authorities for him to be  arrested on the rape charges and  extradited to Sweden previous US charges  accused Assange of conspiring with  Manning to crack a Defense Department  computer password free speech advocates  say the case is part of a wider  crackdown by the Justice Department on  national security leaks  it's got traditional news outlets  worried - searching out and publishing  classified information is part and  parcel of their work those same concerns  led the Obama administration Justice  Department to balk at bringing charges  for similar conduct the new charges  raised immediate questions about media  freedom
 

